In this lesson we'll look at three ideas that quietly run through heaps of everyday maths: ratios, scales, and money. First up, a ratio. Think of it as a way to compare two amounts of the same thing, like saying you and a friend share a pizza in the ratio two to one. To turn that into actual pieces, you split the whole into three equal parts and take two of them. So if there's a total of thirty slices of cake in the ratio two to one, you take two thirds, your friend takes one third. Next, scale drawings. A scale of one to fifty means one centimetre on the page is fifty centimetres in real life. Linear measurements stretch by that factor, but here's the trick: areas stretch by the square of the factor, and volumes by the cube. So a tiny model can represent a huge building, but the surface and the space inside grow much faster than the length. Finally, financial modelling. Most best-buy problems just ask for a unit rate: price divided by weight, or cost per kilometre, or dollars per kilowatt hour. Wages, fuel, electricity, they're all just a unit rate multiplied by how much you use. Now let's see it in action.